::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L02 Q01::Claim: Students should use digital calendars to track assignments. Which evidence best supports the claim? {
~A. Digital calendars can use different colors.#Incorrect. This may describe a feature, but it does not prove the calendar helps track assignments.
=B. A teacher reports fewer missed deadlines when students record due dates in a calendar.#Correct. This evidence directly connects calendar use to assignment tracking.
~C. Calendars have existed for a long time.#Incorrect. This does not support the claim about managing assignments.
~D. Some students prefer paper notebooks.#Incorrect. This may be relevant to a different discussion, but it does not support the claim.
}

::MLA.ENG1.RS.01 U06 L02 Q02::Scenario: A speaker needs evidence about student reading habits. Which source is most reliable? {
~A. A social media post saying reading is boring#Incorrect. A personal post is not reliable evidence for an academic presentation.
~B. A random comment from an unknown user#Incorrect. The source is not verifiable or clearly reliable.
~C. A joke about students and books#Incorrect. A joke does not provide credible evidence.
=D. A school survey summarizing how often students read outside class#Correct. A school survey is relevant and provides usable evidence about the topic.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L02 Q03::Which sequence is best for oral organization? {
=A. Claim, reason, evidence, explanation#Correct. This order gives listeners the point first, then support and meaning.
~B. Evidence, evidence, unrelated story, claim#Incorrect. This delays the claim and includes information that may distract listeners.
~C. Source name, closing, claim, new topic#Incorrect. This order is confusing and does not build support logically.
~D. Explanation, transition, unrelated fact, claim#Incorrect. The claim should come early so listeners know what the evidence supports.
}

::MLA.ENG1.R.06 U06 L02 Q04::Claim: After-school tutoring supports student learning. Which explanation best connects evidence to the claim? Evidence: Sign-in records show that tutoring attendance increases before exams. {
~A. Exams are part of many classes.#Incorrect. This is true but does not explain how the evidence supports the claim.
~B. Tutoring happens after school.#Incorrect. This repeats basic information without connecting evidence to learning.
=C. Higher attendance before exams suggests students use tutoring when they need academic review.#Correct. This explains how the evidence supports the claim.
~D. Sign-in sheets are made of paper or digital forms.#Incorrect. This describes the record but not its meaning.
}

::MLA.ENG1.W.02 U06 L02 Q05::Claim: Students should learn how to check source credibility. Which reason best supports the claim? {
~A. Websites can have different colors and layouts.#Incorrect. Design differences do not explain why credibility checking matters.
=B. Students need to avoid using inaccurate information in assignments and presentations.#Correct. This reason logically supports the claim and prepares for evidence.
~C. Search results appear quickly online.#Incorrect. Speed does not prove that information is trustworthy.
~D. Some web pages have long titles.#Incorrect. Title length does not show why source evaluation matters.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L02 Q06::Scenario: A speaker gives three statistics but never explains them. What is the main problem? {
~A. The speaker has no evidence at all.#Incorrect. The speaker has evidence, but it is not explained.
~B. The speaker used too few numbers.#Incorrect. More numbers would not solve the missing explanation.
~C. The speaker used standard English.#Incorrect. Standard English is not the problem.
=D. The speaker does not connect the evidence to the claim.#Correct. Evidence must be explained so listeners understand why it matters.
}

::MLA.ENG1.RS.01 U06 L02 Q07::Scenario: A presentation needs evidence about school recycling. Which source is most useful? {
=A. A school recycling report showing how much paper was collected each month#Correct. This source is specific, relevant, and likely reliable for the topic.
~B. A student's unrelated story about lunch#Incorrect. The story does not provide evidence about recycling.
~C. A rumor that recycling probably works#Incorrect. A rumor is not reliable evidence.
~D. A picture of a trash can without explanation#Incorrect. A picture alone does not provide clear evidence for the claim.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L02 Q08::Claim: A quiet study room would help students complete assignments. Which oral sentence best explains evidence? {
~A. The study room has walls and lights.#Incorrect. This does not explain how evidence supports the claim.
~B. The room is located near the hallway.#Incorrect. Location alone does not support the claim about completing assignments.
=C. A sign-in log showing heavy use during project weeks suggests students seek quiet space when assignments require focus.#Correct. This sentence explains how the evidence supports the claim.
~D. Study rooms are rooms where people study.#Incorrect. This is circular and does not use evidence.
}

::MLA.ENG1.W.02 U06 L02 Q09::Which pair best shows claim and reason? {
=B. Claim: Students should use planners. Reason: Written due dates help students remember long-term assignments.#Correct. The reason directly supports the claim.
~A. Claim: Planners have pages. Reason: Some pages are small.#Incorrect. This does not support why students should use planners.
~C. Claim: Students use backpacks. Reason: Backpacks have zippers.#Incorrect. The reason does not meaningfully support a position.
~D. Claim: Homework exists. Reason: Pencils write on paper.#Incorrect. The reason is unrelated to the claim.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L02 Q10::Scenario: Which sentence works best as an oral transition after giving evidence? {
~A. Anyway, that is a thing.#Incorrect. This is vague and does not connect ideas clearly.
~B. I found that online somewhere.#Incorrect. This weakens credibility and does not explain meaning.
~C. So, yeah, that happened.#Incorrect. This is informal and does not guide listeners.
=D. This evidence matters because it shows how the strategy helps students manage their work.#Correct. This transition connects evidence back to the claim.
}

::MLA.ENG1.R.06 U06 L02 Q11::Claim: Student-led study groups can support review. Which evidence is strongest? {
=A. A class survey shows students who attended study groups reported feeling more prepared for quizzes.#Correct. This evidence is relevant and directly supports the claim.
~B. Study groups can meet in many places.#Incorrect. This is a general detail, not strong support.
~C. Some students have notebooks.#Incorrect. This does not connect to study groups or review.
~D. The word group has five letters.#Incorrect. This is unrelated to the claim.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L02 Q12::Scenario: A speaker's evidence is credible but unrelated to the claim. What should the speaker do? {
~A. Keep it because all credible evidence is useful.#Incorrect. Credible evidence still must support the claim.
~B. Put it at the beginning so listeners hear it first.#Incorrect. Placement does not fix unrelated evidence.
=C. Replace it with evidence that directly supports the claim.#Correct. Evidence must be both credible and relevant.
~D. Remove the claim and present only the evidence.#Incorrect. The claim is needed to organize the presentation.
}

::MLA.ENG1.RS.01 U06 L02 Q13::Scenario: A student uses a source but cannot identify who created it or where the information came from. What is the concern? {
~A. The source is automatically perfect.#Incorrect. Missing creator or origin information makes the source less trustworthy.
=B. The source may not be reliable enough for an academic presentation.#Correct. Reliable sources should be traceable and credible.
~C. The source must be used because it is short.#Incorrect. Length does not prove reliability.
~D. The source should be used only as the conclusion.#Incorrect. Placement does not solve a reliability problem.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L02 Q14::Which sentence best introduces evidence in an oral presentation? {
~A. Here is some stuff I saw.#Incorrect. This is vague and does not identify the source or evidence clearly.
~B. There are facts about this somewhere.#Incorrect. This weakens credibility because it does not name evidence.
=C. According to a school survey, many students use written reminders to track assignments.#Correct. This identifies the source and presents evidence clearly.
~D. You probably know what I mean already.#Incorrect. This assumes understanding and does not present evidence.
}

::MLA.ENG1.W.02 U06 L02 Q15::Claim: Schools should provide short research workshops. Which evidence best supports the claim? {
=A. Teachers report that students often need help deciding whether online sources are reliable.#Correct. This evidence supports the need for research workshops.
~B. Workshops can happen in rooms.#Incorrect. This is true but does not support the need for workshops.
~C. Research is a word with eight letters.#Incorrect. This is irrelevant to the claim.
~D. Some students like computers.#Incorrect. This does not prove that research workshops are needed.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L02 Q16::Scenario: A student begins with evidence before naming the claim. Why can this confuse listeners? {
~A. Listeners never need evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence is important, but listeners need context for it.
~B. Evidence should always be hidden until the end.#Incorrect. Evidence can appear early, but the claim should be clear.
=C. Listeners may not know what point the evidence is supposed to support.#Correct. Oral presentations need clear organization so evidence has meaning.
~D. Claims are not allowed in oral presentations.#Incorrect. Claims help organize oral communication.
}

::MLA.ENG1.R.06 U06 L02 Q17::Claim: Visual reminders help students remember deadlines. Which evidence is least supportive? {
~A. A classroom calendar shows upcoming assignment dates.#Incorrect. This supports the claim because it is a visual reminder tied to deadlines.
~B. A student survey reports that many students check posted due-date reminders before leaving class.#Incorrect. This supports the claim because it connects visual reminders to student behavior.
~C. A teacher notes that written due dates reduce repeated questions.#Incorrect. This supports the claim because written due dates are visual reminders.
=D. A poster in the hallway lists the lunch menu.#Correct. This is least supportive because it is a visual display but does not connect to assignment deadlines.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L02 Q18::Scenario: Which sentence best explains why evidence matters? {
~A. The evidence is there and it is evidence.#Incorrect. This repeats the word evidence without explaining its connection to the claim.
~B. Sources are sometimes useful for presentations.#Incorrect. This is too general and does not explain a specific piece of evidence.
~C. My evidence is probably fine, so I will move on.#Incorrect. This does not help listeners understand the evidence.
=D. This detail supports the claim because it shows students already use the strategy when assignments become more demanding.#Correct. This explains how the evidence connects to the claim.
}

::MLA.ENG1.RS.01 U06 L02 Q19::Scenario: Which evidence choice should a speaker avoid in an academic presentation? {
=A. An anonymous post with no source information or evidence#Correct. An anonymous post with no source information is not reliable enough for an academic presentation.
~B. A school survey with clear results#Incorrect. A clear school survey can be a relevant and credible source.
~C. A teacher statement connected to classroom observations#Incorrect. A relevant teacher statement can provide useful support.
~D. A school report with dates and collected data#Incorrect. A dated report with data is generally stronger than an anonymous post.
}

::MLA.ENG1.W.02 U06 L02 Q20::Claim: Students should prepare notes before presentations. Which reason best supports the claim? {
~A. Notes can be written in blue or black ink.#Incorrect. Ink color does not explain why notes help presentations.
~B. Some notes are shorter than others.#Incorrect. Length alone does not support the value of preparing notes.
~C. Notes are pieces of paper or digital text.#Incorrect. This defines notes but does not give a reason for using them.
=D. Prepared notes help speakers organize ideas and remember key evidence.#Correct. This reason directly supports the claim.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L02 Q21::Scenario: A speaker has a claim and evidence but no reason. What should the speaker add? {
=B. A sentence explaining why the claim makes sense before presenting the evidence#Correct. The reason bridges the claim and the evidence for listeners.
~A. A second unrelated claim#Incorrect. Another claim would make the presentation less focused.
~C. A joke that replaces the evidence#Incorrect. Humor cannot replace a clear reason in an academic presentation.
~D. A source title without explanation#Incorrect. A source title alone does not provide reasoning.
}

::MLA.ENG1.R.06 U06 L02 Q22::Claim: Students benefit from peer review before submitting writing. Which evidence best supports the claim? {
=A. A writing teacher reports that students often catch unclear sentences after a peer asks questions.#Correct. This directly supports the benefit of peer review.
~B. Peer review happens during class sometimes.#Incorrect. This states when it happens but not why it helps.
~C. Some students use pencils during writing.#Incorrect. This detail is unrelated to peer review benefits.
~D. Writing assignments can be typed or handwritten.#Incorrect. This does not support the claim about peer review.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L02 Q23::Scenario: Which sentence would work best after presenting evidence in a speech? {
~A. That was the evidence, and now there are other things.#Incorrect. This does not explain how the evidence supports the claim.
~B. I found that because I needed something to say.#Incorrect. This weakens credibility and does not explain the evidence.
=C. This example matters because it shows how the strategy helps students solve the problem described in my claim.#Correct. This clearly connects evidence back to the presentation's claim.
~D. The evidence is finished, so I will stop explaining.#Incorrect. Listeners still need explanation of the evidence.
}

::MLA.ENG1.RS.01 U06 L02 Q24::Scenario: A speaker uses a school survey and names when it was collected. Why does that help credibility? {
~A. Dates make every source automatically perfect.#Incorrect. A date helps, but it does not make every source perfect.
~B. The audience no longer needs an explanation.#Incorrect. Credible evidence still needs explanation.
~C. It makes the presentation longer.#Incorrect. Length is not the reason source information matters.
=D. It lets listeners understand where the evidence came from and how current it is.#Correct. Source information helps listeners judge reliability.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L02 Q25::Scenario: Which oral plan is strongest? {
~A. Claim, unrelated fact, another topic, conclusion#Incorrect. This plan does not organize support around one clear point.
=B. Claim, reason, credible evidence, explanation of how the evidence supports the claim#Correct. This plan is logical and gives listeners a complete path through the idea.
~C. Evidence, source name, new claim, no explanation#Incorrect. This plan is incomplete because it does not clearly connect evidence to a claim.
~D. Topic, topic, topic, closing#Incorrect. Repeating a topic does not develop a supported oral presentation.
}
